<article class="env-example" id="B6B15D">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/B6B15D">Example <span data-tag="B6B15D">7.2.1.3</span>.</a> The $\infty $-topos $\SSet $ has homotopy dimension $0$. More generally, if $\calC $ is an $\infty $-category with a final object $1_{\calC }$, then $\calP (\calC )$ has homotopy dimension $\leq 0$. To see this, we first observe that the Yoneda embedding $j: \calC \rightarrow \calP (\calC )$ preserves limits, so that $j(1_{\calC })$ is a final object of $\calP (\calC )$. To prove that $\calP (\calC )$ has homotopy dimension $\leq 0$, we need to show that the functor $\calP (\calC ) \rightarrow \SSet $ corepresented by $j(1_{\calC })$ preserves effective epimorphisms. This functor can be identified with evaluation at $1_{\calC }$. It therefore preserves all limits and colimits, and so carries effective epimorphisms to effective epimorphisms by Proposition <a href="/tag/A9CCE0" data-tag="A9CCE0">6.2.3.7</a>. </p>
